BOOK-CONSENSUS PANEL AUDIT — is the benchmark a clean instrument for timing?

=== 1. CONSENSUS MEMBERSHIP CENSUS (15min clock) ===
  book-side steps 24,963 over 478 games | n_books median 11, min 1, max 11
  within-game breadth range: mean 0.94, median 0 | games with ANY change 42.1%, with >=3 12.3%

  steps with a membership change: 2.2% (533 of 24,485)
  mean |consensus move| when membership CHANGED :  1.35 pts
  mean |consensus move| when membership CONSTANT:  0.12 pts
  ratio: 11.3x
  share of the consensus-change series' total squared variation carried by those
  2.2% of steps: 54.3%

  churn by time-to-start (the headline horizon results live inside 2h):
    window    steps    churn
     0-60m    1,539    0.52%
      1-2h    1,597    0.88%
      2-4h    3,627    1.32%
      4-8h    7,194    1.61%
       >8h   10,528    3.30%

  direction: 391 joins vs 142 exits — books posting lines as the game approaches;
  61% of exits reverse on the very next step (feed flicker, not a book
  withdrawing a line).

15-min panel: 818 games, 44,354 grid steps

=== 2. EVENT-POPULATION CONTAMINATION ===
  base rate of membership change across all 24,456 book steps: 2.1%
   threshold   events  contaminated  enrichment
        1.0p      341         8.5%        4.0x
        1.5p      209        10.0%        4.7x
        2.0p      122        15.6%        7.4x   <- book_moves default
        3.0p       61        18.0%        8.5x
        5.0p       42        23.8%       11.2x

  READ: a membership change is a jump-shaped event, so the bigger the cut the
  larger the artifact share. This is a property of the INSTRUMENT, not of the
  market, and it is why S3 re-detects the events on a constant panel.

