=== 5. WHAT THIS PANEL CANNOT ANSWER ===
  live book rows: 40,152; per-book quotes retained: 0 (`book_price()` averages at ingest and keeps only the mean and n_books).
  EU/sharp per-book tape: 110,212 quotes, 28 books, 5,393 games — but 12.37 distinct timestamps per game:
    a CLOSING CROSS-SECTION, not a time series.
  US retail per-book tape: 51,130 quotes, 11 books, 4,729 games — but 8.25 distinct timestamps per game:
    a CLOSING CROSS-SECTION, not a time series.

  ONE thing the closing cross-section DOES bound: how staggered the field is.
  Within-game spread between the freshest and stalest book: median 2.0 min, p90 6.1 min; only 14.4% of games
  exceed 5 minutes, and per-book mean age spans just 16.9-18.6 min across 11 books.
  At the close the US field is near-synchronous, so smearing is small THERE. It says
  nothing about the wide pre-game window, where the census above shows the churn
  actually lives.

  Consequence, stated plainly: 'does Pinnacle lead Kalshi, and does the
  retail field follow Pinnacle?' is NOT identified by any data this project
  banked, at any resolution. The no-leader result is a result about the
  CONSENSUS, and S1-S4 are what can be said about that instrument's fitness.
  Answering the per-book question needs one row per bookmaker per snapshot —
  the same Odds API call already made, without the averaging step, so the
  marginal credit cost is zero but the panel must be collected forward.

  NOTE: n_books equality is a LOWER BOUND on membership churn — one book
  leaving as another joins is invisible to it. Every contamination figure
  above understates, and every 'clean' subsample still holds some turnover.
